# noramedi-pgbackrest.cron.example — F4-FCR-002
#
# Host target: /etc/cron.d/noramedi-pgbackrest   (root:root, 0644)
#
# NOT INSTALLED. Installing this is the last step of the activation sequence in
# docs/program/runbooks/F4_RECOVERY_OPERATIONS.md §13, after `pgbackrest check`
# has passed. It requires the scoped freeze exception (§14, NOT GRANTED).
#
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ⚠ THIS FILE DOES NOT REPLACE /etc/cron.d/noramedi-db-backup.
#
# The existing pg_dump chain stays exactly as it is:
#
#   15 3 * * * root /usr/local/sbin/noramedi-db-backup.sh
#
# It keeps running at 03:15, keeps writing to /root/noramedi-backups, keeps
# its 7-day retention and its own flock. It is the fallback for the entire
# transition, and removing it is a separate program decision that has not been
# made. Do not edit, disable, or "consolidate" it as part of enabling
# pgBackRest.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# WHY 02:30 AND 02:00:
# Both slots are chosen to clear the 03:15 pg_dump with a wide margin. The
# database is ~16 MB, so a full backup completes in seconds and 45 minutes of
# separation is generous — but the two mechanisms read the same cluster and
# there is no reason to let them overlap while pgBackRest is unproven.
#
# WHY A DAILY FULL RATHER THAN WEEKLY-FULL + DAILY-DIFF:
# Weekly-full/daily-diff is the conventional answer and it is wrong at this
# size. At ~16 MB a full costs seconds, and a full-only chain makes restore a
# single step with no dependency graph to reason about during an incident —
# which matters far more than the storage it saves. Revisit around ~1 GB.
# The wrapper already supports `--type diff` when that day comes.
#
# ⚠ NO OUTER `flock` ON THESE LINES — DELIBERATE. DO NOT "FIX" IT BACK.
#
# The obvious-looking form
#     flock -n /var/lock/noramedi-pgbackrest.lock /usr/local/sbin/…-backup.sh
# is a SELF-DEADLOCK. flock(2) locks belong to the open file description, not
# to the process, so the wrapper would hold the lock on its own fd while the
# script's own independent open() of the same path conflicts with its parent.
# Every scheduled run would exit 5 — "another pgBackRest run holds the lock" —
# and never invoke pgBackRest at all. The failure mode is the dangerous kind:
# cron mails a message that reads like benign overlap while the repository
# silently accumulates nothing, and with archive_mode=on the WAL keeps growing
# on a single 76 GB disk with no backup ever expiring it. The disk-exhaustion
# abort would never be reached either, because the lock check precedes it.
#
# The script owns its lock internally (fd 9, exit 5 on conflict), which is
# exactly why exit 5 is a documented code. The existing pg_dump cron entry has
# no outer flock for the same reason. pgBackRest also takes its own per-stanza
# lock, but that one makes an overlapping run ERROR rather than exit cleanly.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
MAILTO=root

# Daily full backup at 02:30.
# Exit codes the wrapper produces: 0 ok · 1 pgBackRest failed · 2 usage
# · 3 precondition · 4 ABORTED, disk below the free-space floor · 5 lock held.
# Code 4 is the disk-exhaustion abort the freeze exception requires: it means
# pgBackRest was NOT invoked, deliberately, because a full filesystem breaks
# archive_command, which grows pg_wal, which stops PostgreSQL.
30 2 * * *  root  /usr/local/sbin/noramedi-pgbackrest-backup.sh --type full

# Weekly repository integrity verification, Sunday 02:00.
# `pgbackrest verify` checks that what is IN the repository is intact. It is
# NOT a restore test and must never be reported as one — R-032 is closed only
# by an actual restore, which is what noramedi-pgbackrest-restore-drill.sh
# performs. Nothing in the pre-existing pg_dump tier has any equivalent of
# either check.
0 2 * * 0   root  /usr/local/sbin/noramedi-pgbackrest-backup.sh --verify

# ── OFF-HOST (repo2) — COMMENTED OUT AND NOT AUTHORIZED ──────────────────
# Enable ONLY after the repo2 activation sequence in
# F4_RECOVERY_OPERATIONS.md §16.5 has been completed, which itself requires
# the seven §16.2 prerequisites and a procured secondary VPS.
#
# WHY THIS ENTRY MUST EXIST AND NOT BE SKIPPED: `pgbackrest backup` writes to
# ONE repository. archive-push feeds WAL to every configured repository
# automatically, so a repo2 with no scheduled backup still LOOKS alive — it
# accumulates WAL forever on top of a base backup it never received, and
# cannot restore. A single manual backup plus a passing drill is not
# durability either: the off-host proof marker stays valid for 30 days, so
# without a schedule the off-host copy ages out silently behind a green tick.
# opscheck now fails the pitr check on exactly that state (an empty or stale
# repo2), which is what turns a forgotten schedule into an alert instead of a
# discovery during an incident.
#
# Staggered an hour after the repo1 full so the two never contend for the
# stanza lock — the wrapper would exit 5 (lock held) and skip the run.
#30 3 * * *  root  /usr/local/sbin/noramedi-pgbackrest-backup.sh --repo 2 --type full

# Weekly PITR restore drill, Sunday 03:45 — COMMENTED OUT until the first
# manual drill has been run and reviewed by a human.
#
# It restores into a disposable, RAM-backed, SOCKET-ONLY cluster and destroys
# it. Socket-only is literal: the drill pins listen_addresses='' via pg_ctl -o,
# so the restored cluster has NO TCP listener at all — not loopback, not
# anything — and the only pg_hba rule is `local ... peer` over a mode-0700
# socket directory. Earlier revisions of this file described a loopback-bound
# cluster with a `trust` rule; that design was removed in F4-FCR-002A and the
# test suite mutation-tests both defects to prove they cannot come back.
# See F4_RECOVERY_OPERATIONS.md §14.1 for the full safety contract.
#
# It still restores REAL PATIENT DATA, and a physical backup is cluster-wide
# and therefore cross-tenant. Scheduling that unattended before anyone has
# watched it work once is the wrong order. Note also that
# RESTORE_REHEARSAL_REQUIRE_SYNTHETIC has no equivalent here: a physical
# restore cannot be limited to designated synthetic clinics the way the
# file-tier rehearsal can, because there is no per-tenant physical backup.
# Whether rehearsing on real patient data is a distinct processing activity
# requiring its own hukuki sebep is an open COUNSEL_REVIEW_REQUIRED question
# (F4_RECOVERY_OPERATIONS.md §7.3 item 2).
#
# ⚠ BEFORE UNCOMMENTING, SET BOTH VARIABLES. Neither has a default:
#
#   REHEARSAL_OS_USER        the unprivileged OS user the drill drops to. It
#                            must be able to READ the deployed server
#                            directory; the drill checks that BEFORE
#                            restoring, not after.
#   NORAMEDI_APP_SERVER_DIR  the deployed server directory PM2 actually runs
#                            (contains package.json, prisma/migrations and
#                            node_modules/@prisma/client). The application
#                            smoke and the migration-set comparison both read
#                            the DEPLOYED release from it.
#
# The line below carries a <REPLACE> placeholder on purpose. Uncommenting it
# unedited makes the drill abort at preflight with exit 3 having restored
# nothing — fail-closed, by design, because a run that only proves a cluster
# starts is not R-032 evidence. Also install the smoke helper next to the
# drill script (`install -m 0755 scripts/noramedi-pitr-app-smoke.mjs
# /usr/local/sbin/`); the drill refuses to run without it.
#
# 45 3 * * 0  root  REHEARSAL_OS_USER=postgres NORAMEDI_APP_SERVER_DIR=<REPLACE — deployed server dir> /usr/local/sbin/noramedi-pgbackrest-restore-drill.sh --port 55433
